/code-review high — loop U4, round 2, branch 2026-10-19-mobile-toolchain-tasks (main...HEAD = 05839a0)

[
  {"file": "tools/kotlin/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/kotlin", "line": 137, "summary": "The normal (check) run resolves with --write-locks. Gradle then ignores the existing lock state and resolves fresh (unlike --update-locks, which keeps the lock as input), so the run checks whether today's resolution matches the lockfile, not whether the lockfile pins the build.", "failure_scenario": "A project declares a dynamic version (`1.+`, `latest.release`) or a range with a committed lockfile. Upstream publishes a new matching version, the next plain `mise run setup` (including CI) rewrites the lockfile, and the task exits 1 with 'Run setup:deps:kotlin --upgrade', even though the committed lockfile was valid and the build would still resolve against it. Locking exists to make this reproducible."},
  {"file": "tools/android/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/android", "line": 24, "summary": "Android Gradle plugin detection and detect_level only look at *.gradle.kts (and build-logic or the version catalog). A Groovy-DSL Android project (build.gradle with `id 'com.android.application'`) is never detected.", "failure_scenario": "The root has ./gradlew, app/build.gradle with `plugins { id 'com.android.application' }` and `compileSdk 34`, and no version catalog. HAS_AGP stays false, the task exits 0 silently, and no platform or emulator image is installed. code:lint:android does accept build.gradle in module_of, so the two tasks disagree."},
  {"file": "tools/android/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/android", "line": 47, "summary": "The find and grep -r searches prune only build, .gradle, .git and build-logic. They walk .worktrees/ and .claude/worktrees/ (gitignored, nested checkouts in this workflow), plus node_modules and similar folders.", "failure_scenario": "In the main checkout with `.worktrees/feature-x/app/build.gradle.kts` (compileSdk 35) and `./app/build.gradle.kts` (compileSdk 36), `sort` puts `./.worktrees/...` before `./app/...`. The task installs platforms/android-35 and reports it 'from .worktrees/feature-x/app/build.gradle.kts'. AGP detection can also return true because of another branch's checkout, and large trees make every setup slow."},
  {"file": "tools/android/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/android", "line": 172, "summary": "The platform id is never checked against the SDK list (that check was removed in round 2). The run log says `android sdk install` with an unknown id installs nothing and exits 0, so the task prints 'Android SDK packages installed' without the platform.", "failure_scenario": "compileSdk = 37 is normalized to platforms/android-37.0. If the repository names it differently (or COMPILE_SDK has a typo), the install is a no-op with exit 0, the task reports success, and the later Gradle build fails on a missing platform far from the cause."},
  {"file": "tools/swift/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/swift", "line": 46, "summary": "The full-Xcode check (`xcodebuild -version`) runs for every root, including a Package.swift-only root, which needs only `swift` (available from the Command Line Tools).", "failure_scenario": "On a Mac with only the Command Line Tools, a pure SwiftPM package (no *.xcodeproj) fails `mise run setup` with 'Xcode is not selected', although `swift package resolve` would work. The check belongs inside the `${#PROJECTS[@]} -eq 1` branch."},
  {"file": "tools/swift/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/swift", "line": 39, "summary": "On a non-Darwin host the task hard-fails instead of skipping. setup runs every setup:deps:* task in every environment, and base sets os = \"linux\" for ci, prod and stage.", "failure_scenario": "A repository with the swift tool runs `mise run setup` in a Linux CI job (for lint or docs). setup:deps:swift exits 1 with 'Swift is macOS only' and the whole setup fails. swift.md gives no CI note, unlike kotlin.md's Adapt item 2."},
  {"file": "tools/android/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/android", "line": 136, "summary": "The 'installed means no network' shortcut checks only the derived image id. When the _ps16k variant was installed by an earlier run, the original id is never installed, so every run lists the SDK over the network.", "failure_scenario": "With targetSdk 37.1, the first run installs `..._ps16k/arm64-v8a`. Every later `mise run setup` still finds `system-images/android-37.1/google_apis/arm64-v8a` missing, runs `android sdk list --all` (network), and fails offline. This contradicts android.md's 'When all four are installed, the task does not list or install, so it needs no network.'"},
  {"file": "tools/kotlin/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/kotlin", "line": 83, "summary": "The init script iterates the live `confs.matching { it.canBeResolved }` view while calling resolve(). Gradle's own resolveAndLockAll example uses findAll, which takes a snapshot.", "failure_scenario": "A plugin (Kotlin Multiplatform, AGP) adds a configuration lazily while another is resolving. The live iterator throws ConcurrentModificationException or skips the new configuration, so the resolve fails or a lockfile is incomplete. `confs.findAll { it.canBeResolved }.each { it.resolve() }` avoids both."},
  {"file": "tools/android/.config/mise/tasks/code/lint/android", "line": 67, "summary": "The file filter accepts *.gradle.kts but not Groovy *.gradle, and module_of treats only settings.gradle.kts as build-wide, although module_of itself recognizes build.gradle modules.", "failure_scenario": "A commit that changes only `app/build.gradle` (lint options, dependencies) or `android/app/build.gradle` in an older Flutter template does not trigger lint. A change to `settings.gradle` is dropped entirely, so lint regressions from build-file changes pass the pre-commit code:lint."},
  {"file": "tools/kotlin/.config/mise/tasks/setup/deps/kotlin", "line": 177, "summary": "New task output uses print_yellow, which helpers marks 'Kept for the tasks that already call them', instead of a status printer.", "failure_scenario": "Repo CLAUDE.md rule: 'Status lines (`print_wait`, `print_success`, `print_info`, `print_warn`, `print_error`) go under a header or a subheader.' Lines 34, 177 and 181 use a legacy printer outside that set, so the hints carry no [WARN] or [INFO] label in plain-output logs and grep cannot find them. print_info or print_warn would fit."}
